  "name": "Question 6",
  "problem": "For the function $f(x)=x-{e}^{x},$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $x=0$ is a critical point of $f(x)$\n- ${f}^{\u2033}(0)<0$\n- $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=0$",
  "worked_solution": "We need to identify whether $x=0$ is a critical point. If it is, we then need to classify it using the second derivative test.\n\nFirst, we need to identify the critical points. Differentiating $f(x)=x-{e}^{x},$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{\u2032}(x)=1-{e}^{x},\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{\u2032}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-{e}^{x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{x} & =1 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have a critical point at $x=0.$\n\nNext, we need to determine the sign of ${f}^{\u2033}(x)$ at this critical point. Computing ${f}^{\u2033}(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{\u2033}(x)=-{e}^{x},\n$$\n\nand evaluating ${f}^{\u2033}(x)$ at $x=0$, we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{\u2033}(0)=-1<0.\n$$\n\nThe second derivative is negative at $x=0,$ so this critical point is a relative *maximum*. \n\nTherefore, comparing our results with the given statements, we conclude that:\n\n- $x=0$ is a critical point of $f(x)\\,\u2713$\n\n- ${f}^{\u2033}(0)<0\\,\u2713$\n\n- $f(0)$ has a relative *maximum* at $x=0\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"",
